[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 377,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 176,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57802,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 176,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome (PJS) is an autosomal dominant polyp and cancer predisposition syndrome characterized by mucocutaneous melanin pigmentation and gastrointestinal polyposis associated with <em>STK11</em> mutation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 378,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 177,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57803,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 177,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><strong>MIM numbering</strong></p>\r\n<p>175200 Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 379,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 178,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57804,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 178,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LD2D.0 Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 380,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 179,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57805,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 179,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 381,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 180,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57806,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 180,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 382,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 181,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57807,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 181,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Polyps in PJS typically occur in the small intestine (in 95% of PJS patients). About 25% of patients have polyps in the colon and stomach {26721304}. The second most common organ site for malignancy in women is the breast.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 383,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 182,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57808,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 182,
        "headingText": "<p>Presenting symptoms include abdominal pain, intestinal bleeding, anaemia, and intussusception, which typically manifest in the first two decades of life {17311531}. If present, the characteristic mucocutaneous pigmentation allows diagnosis of asymptomatic patients in familial cases, but the characteristic Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps constitute the main clinical hallmark.</p>\r\n<p>PJS is associated with a moderate or high risk of a range of malignancies, with an overall risk of any cancer by the age of 70&nbsp;years of 81% (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/32/138/6871\" target=\"_blank\">#6871</a> Table&nbsp;9.05) {16707622; 11113065; 3587280}. Well-documented extraintestinal tumours include carcinomas of the breast and pancreas, as well as otherwise rare gonadal lesions, including sex cord tumour with annular tubules of the ovary and Sertoli cell tumour of the testis.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-28T17:14:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 384,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 183,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57809,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 183,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The incidence of PJS is roughly one tenth that of familial adenomatous polyposis, with an estimated incidence of 1&nbsp;case per 50&nbsp;000&ndash;200&nbsp;000&nbsp;births.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 385,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 184,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57810,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 184,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A germline mutation in the tumour suppressor gene <em>STK11</em> (formerly <em>LKB1</em>) encoding a serine/threonine kinase can be found in &gt;&nbsp;90% of PJS patients. Most germline defects are point mutations and small intragenic deletions, but larger deletions of one or more exons have also been described {17924967}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 386,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 185,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57811,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 185,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The direct precursor to gastrointestinal cancer in PJS patients remains unknown {16344569}. Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps are probably an epiphenomenon to the cancer-prone condition and not obligate malignant precursors {16344569; 21940722}. There are no data on the pathogenesis of breast cancer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 393,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 206,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57812,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 206,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is not known to be at variance with that of sporadic breast cancer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 387,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 186,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57813,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 186,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are few data on breast cancer phenotypes in PJS, but the very limited data suggest that akin to cancers in <em>BRCA2</em> carriers, breast cancers in PJS are likely to be of intermediate-high grade, ER-positive and PR-positive, and ERBB2 (HER2)-negative {30689838}. Gastrointestinal Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps have been described in detail in the <em>Digestive system tumours</em> volume of this series [[WHO Classification of Tumours Editorial Board. Digestive system tumours. Lyon (France): International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2019. (WHO classification of tumours series, 5th ed.; vol. 1). http://publications.iarc.fr/579.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 388,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 187,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57814,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 187,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The cytology is not known to be at variance with that of sporadic breast cancer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 389,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 188,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57815,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 188,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 392,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 191,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57816,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 191,
        "headingText": "<p>The diagnostic criteria for PJS are presented in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/32/138/6872\" target=\"_blank\">#6872</a>Box&nbsp;9.04 .</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-28T17:14:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 390,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 189,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57817,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 189,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The staging is the same as for sporadic breast cancer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 391,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 190,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57818,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 138,
        "headingId": 190,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The prognosis for patients with PJS is now mainly determined by the risk of malignancy, and an increased cancer mortality has been shown in PJS {21205875}. PJS patients should be surveilled to prevent gastrointestinal complications and cancer {25645574}. Specific data for breast cancer are not available.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-07-30T15:51:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]